      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The largest consumer‑grade above‑ground pools have an internal diameter ≤9 m (30 ft) and a depth ≤2.4 m (8 ft), with walls of steel frame and vinyl liner designed for the pressure of water filling the tank.",
      "evidence": "Product specifications from Intex and Bestway for their 30‑ft round models show max diameter 9 m, depth 1.2–2.4 m, and construction materials rated for the hydrostatic load of ~200 m³ of water.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "A gray whale’s length (≥13 m) exceeds the maximum pool diameter (≤9 m) by at least 4 m, so it cannot be placed lengthwise inside the pool.",
      "evidence": "Direct comparison of the dimensions from steps 1 and 2.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The whale’s maximum body height (~3.5 m) exceeds the deepest residential above‑ground pool (≤2.4 m), so it cannot fit vertically either.",
      "evidence": "Comparison of height from step 1 with depth from step 2.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Even if the pool were enlarged, its wall construction (steel frame with vinyl liner) is engineered for uniform water pressure, not for supporting a 30‑40 t solid mass concentrated in a small area, and would likely collapse under the whale’s weight.",
      "evidence": "Engineering principle: residential pool walls are rated for hydrostatic pressure of water (~1 t/m²) but not for point loads of tens of tons; similar loads would exceed material yield strength.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
